      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-338548",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{\u2033} + 4y = x^{2}e^{2x}, y^{\u2032}(0) = y^{\u2032}(\u03c0) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le \u03c0$\n\nLet $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{\u2032}(0) & y_{2}^{\u2032}(0) \\\\ y_{1}^{\u2032}(\u03c0) & y_{2}^{\u2032}(\u03c0)\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}."
