      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-339169",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+y=x{e}^{3x},\\,{y}^{\u2032}(0)={y}^{\u2032}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{\u2032}(0) & {y}_{2}^{\u2032}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\u2032}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & {y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below **without calculating the solution** to this BVP. \n\n$\\,$ The **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}."
